sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/trabecular-stress-alignment-wolffs-law--grok-4-7--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Stress-adaptive remodelling versus a load-independent rule" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/trabecular-stress-alignment-wolffs-law> ;
    dct:description "This attacks the mechanism in thompson1917-ch16-06 and the reorientation prediction in thompson1917-ch16-07 as a sufficiency question, not as a substitute for the micro-CT test. On a 2D plane-stress mesh of a cantilever and of a femoral-head silhouette, iterate a Huiskes-type density update: the strain-energy density is recomputed by linear finite elements each step, and density grows where strain energy per unit density exceeds a reference value, with a lazy zone of ±10 percent inside which density does not change. The comparison rule uses the same update but drives it with a fixed anatomical gradient and never with stress. After a pre-registered iteration budget, compute the mean-intercept fabric of the density field and its angle to the principal stress of the final solid. Then rotate the load by 30 degrees and continue. A bounded Frontier mechanobiology preview may smoke-test one parameter set; it is not the result, because those previews are not accepted evidence." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Under the stress rule the median fabric-to-stress angle is ≤ 15 degrees, and after the 30 degree load rotation the fabric axis turns by ≥ 20 degrees. The gradient rule stays above 40 degrees from the principal stress. Both comparisons hold for all three initial densities." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The stress rule does not beat the gradient rule by 15 degrees, the density collapses to empty or solid, or the iteration oscillates past the budget without a fabric axis stable to 5 degrees." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/simulation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Freeze the two update rules, the lazy-zone width, the mesh and the iteration budget before any production run. 2. Run the stress rule and the gradient rule from the same initial density. 3. Measure fabric-to-stress angle at convergence. 4. Rotate the load by 30 degrees and record the fabric rotation. 5. Repeat from three initial densities to separate the rule from the initial condition." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: fenics" ;
    skos:note "Tool: python" ;
    skos:note "Tool: frontier_simulate" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-05> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-06> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-16> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/35015116> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/trabecular-stress-alignment-wolffs-law> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Do trabeculae align with principal stress, and is that alignment causal?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1038/35015116> dct:title "Effects of mechanical forces on maintenance and adaptation of form in trabecular bone" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Huiskes, Ruimerman, van Lenthe and Janssen 2000, Effects of mechanical forces on maintenance and adaptation of form in trabecular bone" .
}

